    {
      "id": "u03-l1",
      "title": "Zero to ten",
      "goal": "Count from 0 to 10.",
      "tip": {
        "title": "Silent and spoken endings",
        "body": "Final consonants are usually silent in French, but numbers are an exception: cinq, sept and huit pronounce their final consonant when said alone (but the p of sept is silent: \"set\"). Six and dix end in an \"s\" sound alone (sees, dees), a \"z\" before a vowel (dix_euros) and nothing before a consonant (dix cafés = dee). Un rhymes roughly with \"uh\" through the nose; deux sounds like \"duh\" with rounded lips."
      },
      "phrases": [
